                {
                  "text": "I'm examining how the 5x5 input's nested pattern of 1s and 2s might scale into the 10x10 output, trying to figure out if it's a doubling or a nested L-shape expansion.\n\nI now see it as concentric layers anchored at the top-left corner: output[i][j] equals the color at layer max(i,j), where the layer colors come from the input's first row repeated twice to fill length 10. I've verified this against rows 4 and 5, and now I'm testing whether this same layering rule holds for a different input example with a distinct color sequence.\n\nBut I notice a wrinkle: when the input row contains a zero, the verifier seems to filter it out before building the sequence, which changes the repeat length and pattern \u2014 I need to check if that filtering logic actually applies here.\n\nWorking through the paint step: I upscale the oriented input by 2 to get a 10x10 grid, then for cells above/on the diagonal I overwrite with values from the repeated sequence indexed by column, while cells below the diagonal keep the upscaled input value, and finally I mirror and reorient the result.\n\nI'm mapping the sequence [8,9,5,5,8,9,5,5,8,9] onto the grid so every cell gets seq[max(row,col)], then applying the identity orientation at the end since the smallest object determines the origin \u2014 checking this against the example confirms the output matches.\n\nWith seq = 8,9,5,5,8,9,5,5,8,9, both mirrors together cover the full grid so each cell is seq[max(i,j)]. Checking x38, the origin cell is again an isolated single-cell object making it the smallest, so identity applies there too, and I build the output rows accordingly starting with row 0: 8,9,5,5,8,9...\n\n",
                  "type": "reasoning_text"
                }
